THE DRUGFLUENCER

Written by Sam Kriegman

FADE IN:

EXT. BROOKLYN LOFT - DAY (PRESENT DAY - 2028)

Gray winter light through industrial windows. A film crew sets up: cameras, ring lights, sound equipment. Professional but minimal.

INT. BROOKLYN LOFT - CONTINUOUS

MARCUS HAYES (28) sits in the interview chair. Handsome in an accessible way—the kind of face that photographs well but doesn't intimidate. Well-groomed. Expensive casual: Supreme hoodie, designer jeans. He's done this before.

Behind the camera: INTERVIEWER (30s, tired), and a small crew.

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
Whenever you're ready.

Marcus nods. Takes a breath. Practiced vulnerability.

MARCUS
I loved her. I want to say that first. Before anything else, I loved Zoe.

He looks directly at camera. Holds the moment.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
But I couldn't save her.

TITLE CARD: "THE BOYFRIEND - SIX MONTHS BEFORE"

CUT TO:

INT. KREUZBERG APARTMENT - BERLIN - NIGHT (SIX MONTHS EARLIER)

Small, cluttered, authentically bohemian. Vintage furniture, plants everywhere, film posters in German. Through the window: neon signs reflecting off wet streets.

ZOE WINTERS (26) sits on a worn couch, ring light clipped to a bookshelf, phone mounted on a tripod. She's beautiful in an unfiltered way—no makeup, messy hair, oversized cardigan. The kind of beauty that reads as "real" on camera.

She's mid-stream. On her phone screen: scrolling comments, hearts floating up. 4,847 viewers.

ZOE
(to camera)
—and that's the thing people don't get about harm reduction. It's not about saying drugs are good or bad. It's about acknowledging that people use them, and keeping those people alive.

She takes a sip from a beer bottle. Natural, unselfconscious.

ZOE (CONT'D)
Like, I'm drinking right now. Alcohol is a drug. But we've normalized it, right? We have cultural frameworks for safe use. Don't drive. Drink water. Eat food. We teach people how to drink without dying.

She leans forward, animated.

ZOE (CONT'D)
Why don't we do that with MDMA? With mushrooms? With—

The apartment door opens. MARCUS enters—younger, looser than in the interview. Carries grocery bags. He waves at the camera, mouths "sorry."

ZOE (CONT'D)
(smiling)
That's Marcus. Say hi, babe.

MARCUS
(to camera)
Hey, Zoe's cult.

Comments explode with greetings. Zoe laughs.

ZOE
They're not a cult. They're a community.

MARCUS
A community that watches you every night at—
(checks watch)
—11 PM Berlin time without fail?

ZOE
It's called consistency. It's called showing up for people.

They're flirting. It's genuine. Marcus unpacks groceries in the background while Zoe continues streaming. Easy domesticity.

ZOE (CONT'D)
Anyway. Someone asked about testing kits. Yes. Always. I'll put links in my bio. You can get them mailed discreetly—

Her phone buzzes. She glances down.

ZOE (CONT'D)
Oh shit, we're at 5K viewers. That's a record.

MARCUS
(from kitchen)
You're famous.

ZOE
(to camera, self-deprecating)
I'm really not. But thank you for being here. Truly.

She's sincere. This matters to her.

BACK TO:

INT. BROOKLYN LOFT - DAY (PRESENT)

Marcus in the interview chair.

MARCUS
That was Berlin. That was when it was still... pure, I guess. She genuinely wanted to help people. She'd spent two years working with harm reduction NGOs, needle exchanges. She knew her shit.

Beat.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
The comments were mostly good then. People sharing their stories. People saying she helped them use more safely. Some people saying she helped them quit.

He shifts in his chair.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
The algorithm liked her because she was talking about drugs, but in this... educational way. Flux promoted her content. "Authentic voices." "Real conversations."

His tone sharpens slightly.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
They loved her. Right up until they didn't.

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
When did things change?

Marcus considers this.

MARCUS
I don't know if there was one moment. It was... gradual.

CUT TO:

INT. KREUZBERG APARTMENT - NIGHT (FIVE MONTHS BEFORE)

Zoe at her setup. Now 12,458 viewers. The energy is different—more performative.

ZOE
—so tonight, since you guys asked, I'm going to show you what responsible MDMA use actually looks like.

She holds up a small baggie to the camera.

ZOE (CONT'D)
Already tested it. It's clean. I'm taking a safe dose—120 milligrams. I've eaten. I'm hydrated. I have electrolytes ready.

She's clinical about it, educational.

ZOE (CONT'D)
I'm going to take this, and then I'm going to stay on stream so you can see what a normal roll looks like. Not a rave disaster. Not an overdose. Just... a good, safe experience.

Comments flood in. Mix of excitement and concern.

ZOE (CONT'D)
And yes, I know some of you think this is irresponsible. But you know what's actually irresponsible? People taking drugs without any information. People dying because they don't know what they're doing.

She's defiant now. Righteous.

Marcus watches from the doorway, arms crossed. His expression is unreadable.

ZOE (CONT'D)
Knowledge keeps people alive. So let's normalize talking about this.

She dry-swallows the pill. Looks directly at camera.

ZOE (CONT'D)
See you on the other side.

CUT TO:

SERIES OF SHOTS - ZOE'S STREAM

— 30 minutes in: Zoe noticeably more animated, tactile. Running her hands over the couch fabric. "Oh my god, feel this. I wish you could feel this."

— 60 minutes in: Peak. She's radiant, talking rapidly about love, connection, the beauty of existence. Viewers: 18,234.

— 90 minutes in: Dancing to music we can't hear, lost in it. Marcus joins her. They're genuinely happy.

— 3 hours in: Coming down. She's quieter, reflective. "That was beautiful. That's what it should be like. Safe. Intentional. Conscious."

Comments: thousands of hearts, "this changed my perspective," "thank you for being real," "you're amazing."

But also: "this is fucked up," "reported," "you're promoting drug use to kids."

BACK TO:

INT. BROOKLYN LOFT - DAY (PRESENT)

MARCUS
That stream got 2.3 million views in 24 hours. It was her biggest hit.

Pause.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
And that's when I started to worry. Because she saw those numbers and... something shifted.

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
How so?

MARCUS
She started checking her analytics constantly. How long people watched. When they dropped off. What content performed best.

He runs his hand through his hair.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
And the pattern was clear. The more drugs she did on camera, the more people watched. The more vulnerable she was, the higher the engagement.

Beat.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
The algorithm was training her. And she was a good student.

CUT TO:

INT. KREUZBERG APARTMENT - EARLY MORNING (FOUR MONTHS BEFORE)

Zoe sits at her laptop, Marcus asleep in the background. She's scrolling through analytics dashboards. Her face lit by screen glow.

On screen: graphs showing viewership spikes during drug use, drop-offs during educational segments.

She opens a document: "CONTENT CALENDAR"

Types: "Ketamine education + experience? Psychedelic microdosing series? Cocaine: the professional's drug?"

She stares at the screen. The cursor blinks.

BACK TO:

INT. BROOKLYN LOFT - DAY (PRESENT)

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
Did you try to talk to her about it?

MARCUS
Of course I did.

CUT TO:

INT. KREUZBERG APARTMENT - NIGHT (FOUR MONTHS BEFORE)

Marcus and Zoe in bed. She's on her phone, scrolling comments.

MARCUS
You're reading those again.

ZOE
People are responding. Look at this—
(reads)
"You literally saved my life. I was using alone and didn't know about Narcan. Now I keep it with me."

MARCUS
That's great. Really. But you're also getting—

ZOE
I know what I'm also getting. Death threats. People saying I should kill myself. People reporting my account.

She puts the phone down, looks at him.

ZOE (CONT'D)
But if I help one person. If I keep one person alive. Isn't that worth it?

MARCUS
Of course. But... are you helping people? Or are you—

He stops himself.

ZOE
Say it.

MARCUS
Are you helping people, or are you performing for an audience?

Silence. She stares at him.

ZOE
Fuck you.

MARCUS
Zoe—

ZOE
You don't get it. You sit there and watch me, but you don't actually do anything. I'm out there trying to change how people think about harm reduction, about drug policy, about—

MARCUS
You're getting high on camera for money.

The words hang in the air.

ZOE
(quiet, cold)
Get out.

MARCUS
Babe—

ZOE
Get the fuck out. Go to Max's. Go anywhere. Just leave.

Marcus doesn't move.

ZOE (CONT'D)
I said leave!

He grabs a jacket. Leaves.

Zoe sits alone in the dark. After a moment, she picks up her phone.

Opens Flux. Taps "Go Live."

Within seconds: 234 viewers. 891. 2,456.

ZOE (CONT'D)
(to camera, eyes red)
Hey everyone. Sorry it's late. I just... I need to talk.

The viewers flood in.

BACK TO:

INT. BROOKLYN LOFT - DAY (PRESENT)

Marcus sits forward, elbows on knees.

MARCUS
That's the thing about Flux. About all of them. They're always there. 24/7. Ready to watch. Ready to validate.

Beat.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
I could only be human. I got tired. I got frustrated. I had limits.

He looks at the camera.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
The audience never got tired. They always wanted more.

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
When did you know it was getting dangerous?

Marcus goes very still.

MARCUS
The intervention.

CUT TO:

INT. KREUZBERG APARTMENT - DAY (THREE MONTHS BEFORE)

Bright afternoon light. Too bright for the scene about to unfold.

Zoe sits on the couch. Across from her: Marcus, MAX (Marcus's friend, early 30s), and HANNAH (Zoe's best friend from university, concerned).

The vibe is tense, rehearsed.

MARCUS
We're here because we love you.

Zoe looks at each of them. Her expression: betrayal, fury, fear.

ZOE
Is this an intervention?

HANNAH
We're worried. You're using more. You're streaming while you're—

ZOE
I'm being honest. I'm showing reality. That's the whole fucking point.

MAX
You live-streamed yourself on ketamine for four hours last week. You couldn't speak coherently.

ZOE
And 40,000 people watched. 40,000 people saw what K-holing actually looks like. How many of them will now recognize the signs in a friend? How many—

MARCUS
You're rationalizing.

ZOE
I'm doing important work!

HANNAH
(gently)
Zoe. Sweetheart. You've lost twelve pounds. You're not sleeping. You're taking Adderall to stream, then Xanax to come down, then—

ZOE
I'm managing it.

MARCUS
You're not. And we want to help you stop before—

ZOE
Before what? Before I damage my brand?

The cruelty in her voice surprises everyone, including her.

ZOE (CONT'D)
(softer)
I'm sorry. I'm sorry, that was—

She notices something. Looks at Marcus's pocket. His phone is there, but the angle is wrong. The camera is facing her.

ZOE (CONT'D)
(quiet)
Are you recording this?

Silence.

ZOE (CONT'D)
Marcus. Are you fucking recording this?

MARCUS
No, I—

She lunges, grabs his phone. The screen shows a recording app. Red dot. 28:43 minutes elapsed.

ZOE
You're recording my intervention?!

HANNAH
Zoe, wait—

ZOE
All of you. Get out. Get the fuck out of my house!

MARCUS
We're trying to help—

ZOE
(screaming)
GET OUT!

They leave. Hannah crying. Max stunned. Marcus last, looking back.

The door closes.

Zoe stands alone, shaking. She looks at Marcus's phone still in her hand. Looks at the recording.

28:43 of footage.

She opens Flux. Uploads the file.

Types caption: "When the people closest to you don't understand your work."

Hovers over "POST."

BACK TO:

INT. BROOKLYN LOFT - DAY (PRESENT)

MARCUS
I wasn't trying to—
(stops)
That's a lie. I was recording it. I thought... I thought if she could see herself later, when she was sober, she'd understand.

Beat.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
But she posted it. Edited it first—cut it to make us look controlling, her look sympathetic. It went viral. 6 million views.

He laughs bitterly.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
The comments were about evenly split. Half saying we were right. Half saying we were trying to silence her, control her, that her content was valuable and we were just scared of her honesty.

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
How did that feel?

MARCUS
Like I'd lost her. Like she'd chosen the audience over everyone who actually knew her.

Pause.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
A week later, she moved to London. Her manager—Alex—had been messaging her. Offered to professionalize her content. Brand deals. Speaking engagements. "Take it to the next level."

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
Did you try to stop her?

MARCUS
I begged her not to go. Told her it would only get worse. Told her that turning your life into content doesn't end well.

Beat.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
She said I was holding her back. That I didn't believe in her.

He looks down.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
And then she said... she said if I really loved her, I'd support her growth.

CUT TO:

INT. TEGEL AIRPORT - BERLIN - DAY (THREE MONTHS BEFORE)

Zoe at departure gate. Two suitcases. Alone.

She opens Flux. Films herself.

ZOE
(to camera)
New chapter, everyone. London. Bigger opportunities. Growth.

She's trying to sound excited. It doesn't fully land.

ZOE (CONT'D)
Sometimes you have to leave behind what's comfortable to become who you're meant to be.

Comments flood: "proud of you!" "you deserve this!" "don't let anyone dim your light!"

She smiles at the validation.

But her hand shakes slightly as she boards.

BACK TO:

INT. BROOKLYN LOFT - DAY (PRESENT)

MARCUS
That was the last time I saw her in person. We texted for a few weeks. Then... less. Then barely at all.

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
Did you watch her content?

Long pause.

MARCUS
Every single stream. Every post. I couldn't stop.

He looks directly at camera.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
I watched her get worse in real time. Watched her move to LA. Watched her start slurring her words. Watched the comments turn from concern to... entertainment.

Beat.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
People would comment like "oh here we go" when she'd start obviously high. Or they'd make drinking games out of her tics and verbal loops.

His jaw tightens.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
And I'd sit there in Berlin, watching on my phone, and I'd think: I should do something. I should fly to LA. I should call someone.

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
Why didn't you?

MARCUS
Because she made it very clear she didn't want my help. Because I had my own life to live. Because...

He trails off. Then, honest:

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Because it was easier not to.

Silence.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
The night she died, I was watching. She was live from New York. Some corporate apartment. She was so fucked up she could barely sit upright.

His voice cracks slightly.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
She kept saying "I'm fine, I'm fine, this is fine." And 23,000 people were watching. Some commenting "call 911." Most just... watching.

Beat.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
The stream cut out. I thought she'd just passed out, that she'd wake up embarrassed and apologize tomorrow like always.

He stops. Can't continue for a moment.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
She didn't wake up.

Silence.

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
Do you blame yourself?

MARCUS
Every single day.

Beat.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
But I also blame... everyone. The platform. The followers. The system that made her death profitable content.

He leans back, exhausted.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
You know what the worst part is? After she died, my follower count went up. People found me through her old videos. Through the intervention video.

He laughs bitterly.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
I gained 40,000 followers from my girlfriend's death. And I... I didn't delete my account. I could have. I should have.

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
Why didn't you?

Long pause. He looks away from camera.

MARCUS
I don't know.

Another pause. Then, quieter:

MARCUS (CONT'D)
That's a lie too. I do know.

He looks back at camera.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Because it felt good. The attention. The validation. People reaching out, saying they related to my pain, that they'd been through something similar.

Beat.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
I told myself I was raising awareness. Honoring her memory. But really...

He can't finish. Doesn't need to.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
I'm still doing it. Sitting here. Telling you this story. For your documentary. Which will be content. Which people will watch. Which will probably go viral.

He looks at the interviewer behind camera.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
We're all still using her. Aren't we?

The question hangs.

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
I think we have enough. Thank you, Marcus.

MARCUS
That's it?

INTERVIEWER (O.S.)
That's it.

Marcus stands. The crew starts breaking down equipment around him. He lingers, unsure what to do.

His phone buzzes. He looks at it.

ON PHONE SCREEN: Flux notification. "ZoeWinters fan account posted: 'The Boyfriend Speaks: Exclusive Interview Coming Soon.'"

Marcus stares at it. Then, almost unconsciously, he opens the app. Scrolls.

His face lit by the screen.

Still watching.

FADE TO BLACK.

END ACT ONE

---

TITLE CARD: "THE MANAGER"

FADE IN:

[TO BE CONTINUED IN ACT TWO]
